# A flash card
// A word on its front, what it means on its back.
// It's a two sided view, as Solitaire's cards are:  face up it shows the word, face down what it means.
// A click turns it over.

a "flash card" is a two sided view:
	// The Spanish word, on its front
	- its "word" is text
	// What it means, on its back
	- its "meaning" is text

	// A click turns it over
	- on click: turn it over

	- to draw its front:
		<ui-segment appearance="raised" color="blue" align="center" padding="lg">
			<ui-text size="small" color="grey" all-caps>Spanish</ui-text>
			<ui-space/>
			<ui-text size="huge" bold>{its word}</ui-text>
		</ui-segment>
	- to draw its back:
		<ui-segment appearance="raised" color="green" align="center" padding="lg">
			<ui-text size="small" color="grey" all-caps>What it means</ui-text>
			<ui-space/>
			<ui-text size="huge" bold>{its meaning}</ui-text>
		</ui-segment>

	- to test turning a card over:
		set the card to a new flash card with word = "el gato", meaning = "the cat"
		turn the card face up
		the card is face up should be yes
		turn the card over
		the card is face down should be yes
		turn the card over
		the card is face up should be yes
	- it is clickable

// The cards to learn
a "deck" is a list of flash cards
